WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.450
How many times have you heard someone on the
Internet claim that they are ready to show

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:07.410
you “the ultimate, absolute best way to
learn a language”?

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:12.589
Hi, my name is Luca from LucaLampariello.com
and today I’m going to bust all the myths

00:00:12.589 --> 00:00:17.630
and misconceptions about what people think
are the best ways to learn a language.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:22.010
Then, I’ll show you the truth—the right
language learning method that will help you

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:25.340
personally find the fluency you’ve always
been looking for.

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:31.160
The problem with using the magic word “best”
when it comes to methods, strategies, and

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.860
resources is that “best” is subjective.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:37.890
What is best for you might not be best for
me.

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:41.790
Or worse, there might not be a “best”
at all.

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:44.030
Just “the best we’ve found so far”.

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:47.560
But when people look for advice on how to
do something well, that’s what they ask

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:48.560
for first.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:52.800
They want the best, the fastest, and usually
the cheapest.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:58.120
This goes for anything from learning the guitar,
learning to make money, and yes, of course,

00:00:58.120 --> 00:00:59.580
learning a foreign language.

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:04.070
People are obsessed with “the best”, so
that’s what they look for.

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:08.369
If that’s what you’re looking for, then
I’ve got some unfortunate news:

00:01:08.369 --> 00:01:13.520
After 30 years of learning languages and 10
years of coaching people to do the same, I

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:16.820
am 100% positive when I tell you this:

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:21.509
There is no absolute best and fastest way
to learn a language.

00:01:21.509 --> 00:01:22.509
But wait!

00:01:22.509 --> 00:01:25.850
Before you click away, you should know that
there’s good news, too!

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:29.670
News that will help you master a foreign language,
even sooner than you think:

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:33.899
While there is no absolute best and fastest
way to learn a language, there definitely

00:01:33.899 --> 00:01:38.920
is a best and fastest way FOR YOU, personally,
to learn a language.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:45.299
A method that is tailored to your likes and
dislikes, your learning style, and your schedule.

00:01:45.299 --> 00:01:48.460
What you need to do is figure out what that
method is.

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:51.109
However, you don’t have to do it alone.

00:01:51.109 --> 00:01:53.710
Today, you and I will discover it together.

00:01:53.710 --> 00:02:00.740
A method, built on solid learning principles,
that is customized to you and your circumstances,

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:03.829
and applicable to any language you want to
learn.

00:02:03.829 --> 00:02:09.500
Ultimately, the method you use should have
seven key traits:

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:15.310
It should be unique to your circumstances
It should be enjoyable, in a way that suits

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:19.520
your interests
It should be flexible to different learning

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:25.450
scenarios and environments
It should enable you to learn every single

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:29.690
day
It should be built around comprehensible input

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:39.020
It should incorporate all four major language
skills (reading, writing, listening, and speaking)

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:44.010
It should grow and evolve according to your
own experiences and desires.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:46.590
Let’s look at each of these in more detail:

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:47.590
Trait # 1.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:51.870
Your method should be unique to your circumstances
Have you ever wondered why most people do

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:54.120
not succeed at learning a language at school?

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:59.530
There are many reasons, but one of the most
obvious is this: schools employ a “one size

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:01.710
fits all” method of instruction.

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:06.250
No matter their personal interests, learning
styles, and goals, each student is forced

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:11.110
to listen to the same lectures, use the same
textbook, do the same homework, and receive

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:13.140
the same types of assessments.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:18.390
Now, I know that the classroom environment
is an impractical one in which to expect truly

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:23.380
personalized learning, but the truth is still
the same: everyone learns differently.

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:27.730
How you go about a learning task will have
a huge impact on whether or not you, as an

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:31.910
individual, learn the desired skill, or memorize
the desired information.

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:36.780
And that “how” needs to be aligned with
your personality, taste, and drive as a human

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:37.780
being.

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:42.130
Trait #2: Your method should be enjoyable,
in a way that suits your interests

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:44.200
Let’s go back to that language class for
a second.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:48.800
Let’s say you’re learning French, and
your French teacher loves literature.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:52.940
As a result, all of the course curriculum,
in one way or another, is tied to classic

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:54.680
French novels and short stories.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:58.870
Now, let’s also say that you love French,
too, but you personally hate literature.

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:02.790
To you, there’s nothing more boring than
poring over old books with old characters

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:07.290
using old, flowery language that has nothing
to do with the world you currently live in.

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:10.960
Given your dislike of literature, do you think
you’ll learn as much from your French class

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:16.130
as your classmate, who, let’s say, can quote
Victor Hugo’s Les Miserables from memory?

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:17.130
Probably not.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:22.189
Simply put, you learn best from the things
that you personally find interesting.

00:04:22.189 --> 00:04:27.229
If you’re a big sports fan, you would do
much better in your hypothetical French class

00:04:27.229 --> 00:04:32.070
if you used the language to learn about recent
happenings in France’s national soccer league,

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:33.070
Ligue 1.

00:04:33.070 --> 00:04:36.939
I’m a firm believer that we should learn
language through the topics and activities

00:04:36.939 --> 00:04:38.379
that move us.

00:04:38.379 --> 00:04:39.699
That make us tick.

00:04:39.699 --> 00:04:43.629
If you combine your language learning with
those activities, you’ll have an unbeatable

00:04:43.629 --> 00:04:46.810
combination that will motivate you endlessly.

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:51.469
In addition to enjoying what we learn from,
we have to enjoy how we learn, as well.

00:04:51.469 --> 00:04:56.430
If you see a method touted online as the best-ever
way to learn a language, that might sound

00:04:56.430 --> 00:05:00.900
great, but what if it requires you to learn
in a way that repulses you, or bores you to

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:01.900
death?

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:03.000
Should you push forward?

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:04.000
No!

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:11.169
For your health and sanity, do not force yourself
to use a method you hate, no matter how good

00:05:11.169 --> 00:05:12.750
other people say it is.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:18.189
Trying to do so will only lead to disappointment,
and ultimately burnout.

00:05:18.189 --> 00:05:20.199
This is what I’ve seen as a language coach.

00:05:20.199 --> 00:05:25.430
I’ve spent years developing my Bidirectional
Translation method, a learning strategy that

00:05:25.430 --> 00:05:29.580
I’ve found works brilliantly for me and
most of the students I work with.

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:34.689
However, if a student tries the method, and
doesn’t like it, I don’t push it on them.

00:05:34.689 --> 00:05:37.729
Instead, we move on and try a different method.

00:05:37.729 --> 00:05:42.860
In general, people will stick with the learning
strategy that they like best, not the one

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:45.189
that everyone says is best.

00:05:45.189 --> 00:05:48.680
So find the method you like, and stick with
it.

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:54.360
Trait 3: Your method should be flexible to
different learning scenarios and environments

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:59.180
When I first developed my Bidirectional Translation
method, I applied it to each and every language

00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:00.500
I decided to learn.

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:02.319
For the most part, it worked great!

00:06:02.319 --> 00:06:05.879
I had amazing success using the method with
ten languages.

00:06:05.879 --> 00:06:11.210
However, when I tried using the method with
Japanese, something entirely different happened—it

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:12.689
didn’t work.

00:06:12.689 --> 00:06:15.650
I hit a wall, and couldn’t make progress.

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:18.689
Stubborn, I decided to try to push forward
anyway.

00:06:18.689 --> 00:06:23.650
But no matter how hard I tried to apply and
reapply my tried-and-tested Bidirectional

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:26.699
Translation method, I couldn’t get anywhere.

00:06:26.699 --> 00:06:30.150
In hindsight, what I should have done then
was be flexible.

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:35.469
I should have taken my Bidirectional Translation
method and attempted to adapt it to better

00:06:35.469 --> 00:06:38.530
suit Japanese, rather than the other way around.

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:45.480
Instead, I remained rigid and inflexible in
my learning strategies, and that’s why I

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:46.480
failed.

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:48.900
Here’s how you can stay flexible in your
approach to learning, even in those times

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:49.900
when you hit a wall.

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:53.940
Once a month, stop and engage in a self-reflection
exercise.

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:56.639
Ask yourself “is what I’m doing enjoyable?

00:06:56.639 --> 00:06:57.979
Is it working well for me?

00:06:57.979 --> 00:07:01.909
Do I wake up every day wanting to engage in
my learning?

00:07:01.909 --> 00:07:05.999
If not, how can I change things so that the
answers to the above questions can all be

00:07:05.999 --> 00:07:06.999
“yes”?

00:07:06.999 --> 00:07:11.300
Answering these questions allows you to readjust
and reassess your learning methods, making

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:14.360
them stronger and more adaptable in the long
run.

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:20.229
Trait #4 Your method should enable you to
learn every single day

00:07:20.229 --> 00:07:25.069
This is something I say in every video, interview,
and blog post, ad nauseam:

00:07:25.069 --> 00:07:30.169
If you want to learn effectively, make sure
you learn every day.

00:07:30.169 --> 00:07:35.439
If you can do this, then time will become
your greatest ally in your language learning

00:07:35.439 --> 00:07:36.439
progress.

00:07:36.439 --> 00:07:37.439
Why?

00:07:37.439 --> 00:07:41.979
Because if you can learn every day, even for
just 20 minutes at a time, you will be aided

00:07:41.979 --> 00:07:44.409
by the so-called “compound effect”.

00:07:44.409 --> 00:07:51.050
The gains you make every single day will slowly
but surely accumulate, like a snowball rolling

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:52.759
down a mountainside.

00:07:52.759 --> 00:07:58.689
Pretty soon, what was once a tiny snowball
will become something much, much larger.

00:07:58.689 --> 00:08:02.610
When this concept is applied to language learning,
you’ll see results that are even beyond

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:04.460
your wildest expectations.

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:08.020
I’ll never forget when a friend of mine
called me with amazing news.

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:14.029
He had been learning Japanese for a long time
without much success, but one day, he decided

00:08:14.029 --> 00:08:18.270
to take my advice, and commit to learning
for at least one hour every day.

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:19.310
The news?

00:08:19.310 --> 00:08:23.199
After only six months, he managed to even
have an hour-long conversation with native

00:08:23.199 --> 00:08:27.740
speakers, something that had previously only
been a dream to him.

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:32.169
Keep this critical idea in mind at all times:
language learning is not something that you

00:08:32.169 --> 00:08:38.099
do just for a few weeks, or months; instead,
it is a skill for life.

00:08:38.099 --> 00:08:41.529
Learning every day will help you make that
happen.

00:08:41.529 --> 00:08:45.339
Trait # 5 Your method should be built around
comprehensible input

00:08:45.339 --> 00:08:48.940
If you’ve ever attempted to learn a language
before, then you probably know the feeling

00:08:48.940 --> 00:08:53.370
of learning for a few months, and then attempting
to watch a movie in your target language to

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:55.360
see how your skills match up.

00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:59.790
Even with subtitles of any kind, I’m sure
you found that you understood way less than

00:08:59.790 --> 00:09:00.790
you expected.

00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:05.390
I’m also sure you found it overwhelming,
and perhaps a bit discouraging.

00:09:05.390 --> 00:09:09.410
First things first, let me tell you that it’s
extremely normal to feel that way.

00:09:09.410 --> 00:09:13.650
Native-level, authentic content is extremely
difficult, and to understand it, your brain

00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:17.259
needs massive quantities of input over a long,
long time.

00:09:17.259 --> 00:09:20.500
So how do you get there, especially from where
you are now?

00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:24.320
How do you go from understanding nothing,
to understanding nearly everything?

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:28.540
Here’s the answer: You must get a lot of
comprehensible input.

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:33.190
As you learn new words and phrases, you must
digest content that features those words and

00:09:33.190 --> 00:09:36.329
phrases, as well as some things you’ve never
seen before.

00:09:36.329 --> 00:09:41.910
What you already know will help to serve as
context for the new, unknown elements, and

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:45.459
gradually you’ll figure out what the new
stuff means as well.

00:09:45.459 --> 00:09:47.740
This is the cycle of comprehensible input.

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:51.959
You learn from what you can mostly understand
until you can understand all of it.

00:09:51.959 --> 00:09:54.959
And then you move on to the next thing you
can mostly understand.

00:09:54.959 --> 00:10:01.540
To start, I personally recommend anything
with both printed text and audio, and even

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:04.009
audio-visual materials with subtitles.

00:10:04.009 --> 00:10:09.399
Other beneficial features include simple and
clear grammar notes and explanations, vocabulary

00:10:09.399 --> 00:10:12.860
lists, or even full translations into your
native language.

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:17.980
This kind of material facilitates your quick
understanding of the content, and as a consequence

00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:21.339
makes it more enjoyable and fun to learn with.

00:10:21.339 --> 00:10:25.370
And with enough comprehensible input at the
early stages, it won’t be long until you

00:10:25.370 --> 00:10:30.370
can venture into content that’s specifically
meant for native speakers to enjoy.

00:10:30.370 --> 00:10:36.190
Trait # 6: Your method should incorporate
all four major language skills (reading, writing,

00:10:36.190 --> 00:10:37.840
listening, and speaking)

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:41.220
A lot of language learning methods focus on
just one skill.

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:45.829
For example, you can easily find courses all
about speaking, that endeavor to help you

00:10:45.829 --> 00:10:48.240
improve in just that one area.

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:52.810
However, knowing a language is not about mastering
one skill, but many.

00:10:52.810 --> 00:10:58.440
In particular, there are four major language
skills, which include reading, writing, listening,

00:10:58.440 --> 00:10:59.540
and speaking.

00:10:59.540 --> 00:11:05.020
Over the years, I’ve had many students come
to me after years of focusing on just one

00:11:05.020 --> 00:11:06.699
of the above language skills.

00:11:06.699 --> 00:11:12.100
They want me to help me “fix” their language
abilities, which have now become patchy and

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:13.100
unbalanced.

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:15.470
I’ve seen many examples of this, really.

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:18.080
Someone who can listen well, but can’t speak
even a few words.

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:21.759
Someone who can read well, but can’t follow
a conversation.

00:11:21.759 --> 00:11:25.259
Stories like this exist in all combinations.

00:11:25.259 --> 00:11:29.690
After a short inquiry, it always turns out
that their unbalanced skills reflect the choices

00:11:29.690 --> 00:11:31.191
they made along their learning journey.

00:11:31.191 --> 00:11:35.770
For instance, an American student of mine
told me that he could read well, and listen

00:11:35.770 --> 00:11:37.970
to the radio, but couldn’t speak at all.

00:11:37.970 --> 00:11:43.480
Naturally, I asked him how he regularly practiced
his speaking skills, and...well, he actually

00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:45.760
had never spoken Spanish at all.

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:50.649
Somewhere, deep down, he just hoped that after
enough listening and reading, his ability

00:11:50.649 --> 00:11:52.720
to speak would come naturally.

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:53.720
It didn’t.

00:11:53.720 --> 00:11:57.400
Here’s a bit of knowledge you should always
keep in mind as you learn: if you don’t

00:11:57.400 --> 00:11:59.960
practice a skill, you won’t learn it.

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:05.730
So, naturally, to develop all four skills,
you need to practice all four skills.

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:10.500
This doesn’t necessarily mean dividing your
learning time in four and devoting one chunk

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:14.290
of time to reading, speaking, and so on.

00:12:14.290 --> 00:12:18.980
But it does mean that you have to work on
developing all of these skills over the course

00:12:18.980 --> 00:12:21.630
of a week, a month, or even a year.

00:12:21.630 --> 00:12:26.550
With time, you’ll find that practicing all
four skills together will help them reinforce

00:12:26.550 --> 00:12:31.699
one another, strengthening your brain’s
ability to use and absorb the language in

00:12:31.699 --> 00:12:32.699
amazing ways.

00:12:32.699 --> 00:12:38.660
Trait #7 Your method should grow and evolve
according to your own experiences and desires.

00:12:38.660 --> 00:12:43.100
Whatever language learning method you ultimately
choose, it should not be something that is

00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:46.079
decided for you by someone else.

00:12:46.079 --> 00:12:51.470
Instead, it should come directly from your
tastes, desires, and goals as a learner.

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:55.980
Other people’s advice can be helpful, but
always keep in mind that no one is going to

00:12:55.980 --> 00:12:57.810
learn your target language for you.

00:12:57.810 --> 00:13:02.340
Your language skills are your responsibility,
so how your method is structured, and how

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:07.449
it grows and evolves over time should also
be your responsibility—and yours alone.

00:13:07.449 --> 00:13:11.510
It’s like Steve Jobs said in his famous
Stanford commencement address: “Don’t

00:13:11.510 --> 00:13:16.540
let the noise of others’ opinions drown
out your own inner voice”.

00:13:16.540 --> 00:13:17.810
This is true for everything.

00:13:17.810 --> 00:13:22.120
The internet is full of loud and convincing
voices about language learning—mine included!

00:13:22.120 --> 00:13:24.690
Listen carefully, but take everything with
a grain of salt.

00:13:24.690 --> 00:13:26.949
When you like something you hear, write it
down.

00:13:26.949 --> 00:13:28.250
Test it out.

00:13:28.250 --> 00:13:31.430
Compare it with other advice, and see how
it stacks up.

00:13:31.430 --> 00:13:36.790
Draw from your common sense and experience,
and ultimately integrate the things that resonate

00:13:36.790 --> 00:13:37.790
with you.

00:13:37.790 --> 00:13:38.950
You can discard the rest.

00:13:38.950 --> 00:13:42.379
So there you have it, those are the seven
traits of the language learning method that

00:13:42.379 --> 00:13:44.120
will work best for you.

00:13:44.120 --> 00:13:47.740
Not me, not your teachers, or friends—you!

00:13:47.740 --> 00:13:51.089
If you want more information, check the links
down in the description box.

00:13:51.089 --> 00:13:56.329
I’ve left lots of links to some great resources,
including a blog post I’ve written on this

00:13:56.329 --> 00:13:57.750
exact topic.

00:13:57.750 --> 00:14:03.009
If you enjoyed this video, be sure to like,
subscribe and hit the notification bell for

00:14:03.009 --> 00:14:05.930
many more great videos!

00:14:05.930 --> 00:14:15.490
And as always, thanks for watching!

